I wrote `polyglot/main.rs` and ran both of your commands. `rustc` and `g++` builds print the same results for N = 0, 1, 2, 3, 10, 50 and 90 (1, 1, 2, 3, 89, 20365011074 and 4660046610375530309).

```rust
/* /* */
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstdlib>
int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    unsigned long long n = argc > 1 ? strtoull(argv[1], 0, 10) : 0, a = 1, b = 1;
    for (unsigned long long i = 0; i < n; i++) { unsigned long long t = a + b; a = b; b = t; }
    printf("%llu\n", a);
    return 0;
}
// */
// \
fn main() { let n: u64 = std::env::args().nth(1).and_then(|s| s.trim().parse().ok()).unwrap_or(0); let (mut a, mut b) = (1u64, 1u64); for _ in 0..n { let t = a.wrapping_add(b); a = b; b = t; } println!("{}", a); }
```

How it works:
- **Rust:** block comments can nest. `/* /* */` opens two comments and closes one, so everything stays commented out until the `*/` in `// */`. The line `// \` is then just an ordinary comment, so the Rust `fn main` is compiled.
- **C++:** block comments don't nest, so the first `*/` on line 1 ends the comment and the C++ code is compiled. In `// \`, the backslash at the end of the line joins the next line onto the comment, which hides the Rust code.

Both versions use 64-bit unsigned integers, so results are exact up to N = 92. Above that the numbers overflow and the output is wrong.
